  OBJECT                  = INSTRUMENT_HOST_INFORMATION
    INSTRUMENT_HOST_NAME  = SELENE
    INSTRUMENT_HOST_TYPE  = SPACECRAFT
    INSTRUMENT_HOST_DESC  = "

    SELENE Systems
    ==============

    SELENE satellite consists of main orbiter, relay-satellite, and VRAD 
    satellite. 
    Main orbiter is composed with upper module and lower module.

    System Configuration 
    --------------------

    * Overall systems

    Launch Period
        2007
    Launch Rocket
        H-IIA Single Launch
        4S Fairing    
        2360SA PAF
    System Configuration
        Main Orbiter, Upper Module and Lower Module
        Relay sub-satellite (Rstar)
        VRAD sub-satellite (Vstar)
    Mission Period
        1 year nominal
    Mass
        Total Launch Mass: 2,885 kg (TBD)
        Main Orbiter: 1,732 kg (TBD)
          Fuel      : 1,045 kg (TBD)
        Rstar       : 54 kg (TBD)
        Vstar       : 54 kg (TBD)
    Electric Power
      Main Orbiter
        Generated   b=0  deg  >= 3,480 W
                    b=30 deg     4,000 W
                    b=90 deg  >= 1,930 W
        Consumed    b=0  deg     3,090 W
        (nominal)   b=90 deg     1,930 W
      Relay satellite
        Generated  >=70 W
        Consumed     47 W
      VRAD satellite
        Generated  >=70 W
        Consumed     47 W
    Ground Station
        Tracking; JAXA GN, UDSC (backup), DSN (critical) 
        Data acquisition; UDSC, USC (backup), ESTRACK(TBD)
        SELENE Operation and Data Analysis Center (SOAC) 
        VLBI ground stations

    * Science Instruments

    Main Orbiter
        X-ray Spectrometer (XRS)                           :  21.9 kg
        Gamma-ray/Charged Particle Spectrometer (GRS/CPS)  :  52.5 kg
        Lunar Imager/Spectrometer (LISM)                   :  54.0 kg
        Lunar Radar Sounder (LRS)                          :  22.8 kg
        Laser Altimeter (LALT)                             :  20.0 kg
        Magnetic Field and Plasma measurements (MAP)       :  38.3 kg
        Upper-atmosphere and Plasma Imager (UPI)           :  42.0 kg
        Transponder opposed to Rstar (RSAT-2)              :   3.9 kg
        High Definition Television System (HDTV)           :  16.5 kg
        Total                                              : 271.9 kg
    Relay satellite
        Relay Satellite Transponder (RSAT)                 :  12.8 kg
        Differential VLBI Radio Source (VRAD-1)            :   2.2 kg
    VRAD satellite
        Differential VLBI Radio Source (VRAD-2)            :  10.2 kg

    Configuration of Main Orbiter
    -----------------------------

    * Main Orbiter System

    Nominal Orbit
        Altitude : 100+/-30 km
        Inclination : 90 deg. Circular
        Period : ca. 118 min.
    Attitude Control
        Nominal Mission
            3-axis Stabilized, +Z axis align; normal of lunar surface,
            X-Z axis; orbital plane
        Yaw-around
            2 times/year at b=0 deg
    Mission Period
            One year; nominal mission operation

    * Bus Systems

    Structure
        Type
            Panel
        Outer Dimension
            Upper module: 2.1 X 2.1 X 2.8 m3
            Lower module: 2.1 X 2.1 X 1.4 m3
            Adopter truss: 2.2 m octagonal prism X 0.6 m
    Thermal Control
        Type
            Passive/Active
        Heat Radiation Plane
            Lunar Opposition Plane/ Opposition Plane of
            Solar Paddle Support
        T/C for Science Instruments      
            Exposed Equipments: independent
            Inner Electronics Equipments: dependent
        Components
            Heater, MLI, Thermal Lubber, Thermal Dabbler, OSR etc
    Attitude-orbital Control 
        Type
            3-axis Stabilized
        Nominal Circum Lunar Orbit
            Attitude Control Accuracy: +/- 0.1 deg. In each axis 
            Attitude Stability: +/- 0.003 deg./s in each axis 
            Attitude Knowledge Accuracy: +/- 0.025 deg. In each axis 
        AOCE Faculty
            Lunar Orbit Insertion Control
            Circum Lunar Attitude Control
            Circum Lunar Orbital Control
        Sensors
            Solar Sensor, Star Sensor, Inertial Measurement Unit
        Actuators
            Wheel, Thruster
    Electric Power
        Bus type
            Non-stabilized Single Bus
        Bus Voltage
            Sunny; 50.9 - 52.8 V, Eclipse; 32.6 - 51.2 V
        Battery Capacity
            Ni-Cd 35 Ah,  4 units
                
    * Bus Sub-system

    Solar Cell Paddle
        Paddle Type
            1 Wing, 1-axis Rotating, Fixed Cant
        Paddle Generated Power
            b=0 deg.: 3,480 W (EOL)
            b=90 deg.: 1,930 W (EOL)
        Paddle Size
            22.6 m2 (2385X3159 3 plates)
        Cell Type
            Dual Junction Solar Battery Cell
    Communication
        Frequency
            X-band (Mission Telemetry),
            S-band (Command/Bus Telemetry)
        Transmission Rate
            Commands: 1000 bps
            Telemetry:  2 kbps for Low-bit rate, 
                       40 kbps for High-bit rate.
            Mission Data: 10 Mbps
        Antenna Configuration
            160 cm diameter. High Gain Antenna (S/X common used)
            Omni-directional Antenna for S-band; 4 pieces
    Data Handling
        Encoding Scheme
            Applicable CCSDS Standards
        Data-bus Scheme
            MIL-1553B Standards
        Data Recorder
            MDR: 100 Gbits Memory Capacity
    Propulsion
        Method
            Dual-liquids Mode (ME Pressure Control/ RCS Blow Down) 
        Propulsion liquids
            NTO/N2H4
        Thruster Configuration
            500N (2 liquids) Thruster:  1 Piece
             20N (1 liquid)  Thruster: 12 Pieces
              1N (1 liquid)  Thruster:  8 Pieces
        Propulsion Vessel
            N2H4: 820 mm Diameter/1170 mm Length 2 Pieces
            NTO:  820 mm Diameter Sphere 1 Piece
            GHe:  406 mm Diameter/ 660 mm Length 2 Pieces
        Mass
            N2H4: 710 kg (Nominal)
            NTO:  330 kg (Nominal)
            GHe:  5.4 kg (TBD)
    Monitor Camera
        CCD Camera 4 Pieces
    Integration
        Electrical Integration
            Cabling
        Mechanical Integration
            Brackets

    Configuration of Relay Satellite (Rstar)
    ----------------------------------------

    * Relay-satellite System

    Mission Orbit at Separation from Main Orbiter
        100 X 2400 km, elliptical, Inclination 90 deg., Orbital Period 240
        minutes
    Mission Period
        Nominal One Year
    Attitude Control
        Spin-stabilized

    * Relay-satellite Bus Subsystem

    Power Supply
        Components Power
            Control Unit (PCU), Battery (BAT)
        Bus Voltage
            Assembled: +0 Volt, -1.5 Volt
            Separated: 35 Volt
        Battery Capacity Ni - MH, 13AH 1 series
        Before separation electric power is supplied from upper module.
    Thermal Control
        Method
            Passive/Active Parallel Use
        Heat Radiation
             +/-X plane, Upper/Lower Decks
        Thermal Control of Science Instruments
            Inner equipment electronic dependently controlled
            Antenna equipped outside independently controlled
        Components
            Heater, MLI, Ag-Teflon sheet, etc
    Structure
        Type
            Octahedral Panel Structure 
        Outer Dimension
            990 X 990 X 650 mm Octahedral Prism
        IF with Upper Module
            Separation Mechanism
        Separation Mechanism
            Constant Tension Spring / Guide-roll up
        S/M Dimension
            500 diameter X 150 mm Length Cylindrical
    Integration
        Components
            Umbilical Separation Mechanism, Fastener, Cable, Counter-weight

    Configuration of VRAD Satellite (Vstar)
    ----------------------------------------

    * VRAD-satellite System

    Mission Orbit at Separation from Main Orbiter
        100 X 800 km, elliptical, Inclination 90 deg., Orbital Period 150 
        minutes
    Mission Period
        Nominal One Year
    Attitude Control
        Spin-stabilized

    * VRAD-satellite Bus Subsystem

    Power Supply
        Components
            Power Control Unit (PCU), Battery (BAT)
        Bus Voltage
            Assembled: +0 Volt, -1.5 Volt
            Separated: 35 Volt
        Battery Capacity
            Ni - MH, 13AH 1 series
            Before separation electric power is supplied from upper module.
    Thermal Control
        Method
            Passive/Active Parallel Use
        Heat Radiation
             +/-X plane, Upper/Lower Decks
        Thermal Control of Science Instruments
            Inner equipment electronic dependently controlled
            Antenna equipped outside independently controlled
        Components
            Heater, MLI, Ag-Teflon sheet, etc
    Structure
        Type
            Octahedral Panel Structure 
        Outer Dimension
            990 X 990 X 650 mm Octahedral Prism
        IF with Upper Module
            Separation Mechanism
        Separation Mechanism
            Constant Tension Spring / Guide-roll up
        S/M Dimension
            500 diameter X 150 mm Length Cylindrical
    Integration
        Components
            Umbilical Separation Mechanism, Fastener, Cable, Counter-weight


    Spacecraft Bus Subsystems
    =========================

    Mechanical Structure Subsystem (STR)
    ------------------------------------
    Structure subsystem (STR) is a basic structure element of SELENE spacecraft, 
    which has faculty of sustaining and holding all payloads including the solar 
    paddle, antennas and outside components in allover operation period from 
    ground tests to orbital operation.
    STR is composed of Upper Module and Lower Module, which is separable in the 
    ground tests. 

    Thermal Control Subsystem (TCS)
    -------------------------------
    TCS will keep spacecraft components in predominant ranges of temperature 
    from launch to end of mission using parallel passive thermal control scheme 
    and active one. SELENE will realize thermal control under saving electric 
    power and mass resourceÅAalthough spacecrafts receive characteristic lunar 
    environments of strong infrared radiation from lunar surface, and long 
    eclipse entering into Earth's shadow, which the spacecrafts orbiting the 
    Earth never experience in their orbits.  
    The SELENE spacecrafts will plan to be operated autonomously using onboard 
    controlled heaters.

    Attitude and Orbit Control Subsystem (AOCS)
    -------------------------------------------
    The AOCS will provide attitude acquisition and control using Sun Sensor and 
    Star Tracker scanned by the spacecraft rotation motion, and control pointing 
    of the HGA and the solar paddle. In the cruising and mapping orbit the Star 
    Tracker and the Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) will provide attitude 
    controls and knowledge. 
    During the lunar orbiting phase, nadir orientation of the spacecraft will be 
    maintained as specified below. The AOCS will provide a spacecraft attitude 
    that supports continuous and simultaneous data taking by all science 
    instruments. The AOCS will also be capable of pointing the body mounted 
    science instruments at arbitrary targets in the celestial frame.
    The fundamental reference direction is nadir direction. The +Z axis (nadir 
    direction) is defined by a line passing through the spacecraft perpendicular 
    to the lunar reference spheroid. The +X axis is defined to lie in a plane 
    perpendicular to the nadir direction and along the projection of the 
    velocity vector on this plane. The +Y axis also lies in this plane and is 
    orthogonal to both the +Z and +X axes, forming a right-handed coordinate 
    system. As defined, the +X axis will be close to, but not always coincident 
    with, the direction of the spacecraft velocity vector, and the +Y axis will 
    be close to, but not always coincident with, the orbit normal.

"
  END_OBJECT         = INSTRUMENT_HOST_INFORMATION
END_OBJECT           = INSTRUMENT_HOST

END